{"run_id":"b0443699-0c37-4e10-a44f-712b80bdc4e7","case_id":"R01","arm":"production","repeat":0,"source_sha":"3d9299b01e973a843976ad13dcae9b544e217db4","config_hash":"9a4662ae24e03d0f5a8bb8cc8fc4f8ca2f040f23293385fe8fd36a0b0b859915","corpus_hash":"8be6cf66829e42c59cbec66896d1c762de130283989623a05edc30b427246720","gold_hash":"575cfd2b66465633f7cb04b44eb8455293c1a9c24a4911c0482879313d9b82cb","pricing_hash":"d1a472e5668c0455e1ff2e5a881403e9ccf01e148552153a757bd6c3aabd7f31","attempt_id":"5e12d6e3-2998-4f36-a163-47373f65d92d","trace_ids":["545fcea2-3164-4c0e-bf5e-9b4238f22856"],"retrieved_ids":["7621f3cdfae14d92b5777d44f620edbd19bd2126d494dddfc68261583181ea29","2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf","6ebf013239401d00cc45d78657ddfba061ca9251b07c54795e6171d66b1e8b71","ef2c8278e771d0c5485f4ed65142dbdde28e0ffd29163920c27e96b4c9f30509","ef832f209b0ddce5e6d3d7d1172f5781b55cc9699aeb569c88ee258082bfa41e"],"foundation_evidence_ids":[],"initial_context_evidence_ids":["7621f3cdfae14d92b5777d44f620edbd19bd2126d494dddfc68261583181ea29","2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf","6ebf013239401d00cc45d78657ddfba061ca9251b07c54795e6171d66b1e8b71","ef2c8278e771d0c5485f4ed65142dbdde28e0ffd29163920c27e96b4c9f30509","ef832f209b0ddce5e6d3d7d1172f5781b55cc9699aeb569c88ee258082bfa41e"],"response":{"attempt_id":"5e12d6e3-2998-4f36-a163-47373f65d92d","trace_id":"545fcea2-3164-4c0e-bf5e-9b4238f22856","status":"answered","answer":"A landlord must give a tenant at least 90 days' written notice before increasing rent. The notice must be in a form approved by the Landlord and Tenant Board and must set out the landlord's intention to increase the rent and the amount of the new rent.\nIn most cases, rent can be increased only if at least 12 months have passed since the tenant first moved into the rental unit or since the tenant's last rent increase. If the previous tenant assigned the rental unit to the current tenant within the 12 months before the increase date, the landlord can increase the rent if at least 12 months have passed since the previous tenant's last rent increase.\nFor a controlled rental unit in 2026, a landlord cannot increase rent by more than the guideline of 2.1% per year, except in accordance with specific statutory exceptions. The proposed rent of $1021.00 (an increase from $1000.00) equals exactly 2.1% and complies with the guideline limit.\nNotice was served by hand on June 3, 2026, for an effective date of September 1, 2026, providing 90 days' notice as required. The 12-month spacing requirement is satisfied because the tenancy began September 1, 2025, and no previous rent increase occurred, so the increase on September 1, 2026, is at least 12 months from the tenancy start date.\nForm N1 is the correct notice form for this rent increase because the rental unit is subject to the rent increase guideline under the Residential Tenancies Act and is not exempt or partially exempt.","statements":[{"id":"s1","text":"A landlord must give a tenant at least 90 days' written notice before increasing rent. The notice must be in a form approved by the Landlord and Tenant Board and must set out the landlord's intention to increase the rent and the amount of the new rent.","citation_ids":["9dc1e76e7aa94913d4d506df9ae748a0ae9b8de10a0a9253069dec8ea3bdc163"]},{"id":"s2","text":"In most cases, rent can be increased only if at least 12 months have passed since the tenant first moved into the rental unit or since the tenant's last rent increase. If the previous tenant assigned the rental unit to the current tenant within the 12 months before the increase date, the landlord can increase the rent if at least 12 months have passed since the previous tenant's last rent increase.","citation_ids":["d23348353fecdb08307c3e74b0e8899d26b35fd5bb14420c1f744ed51dbfca52","2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf"]},{"id":"s3","text":"For a controlled rental unit in 2026, a landlord cannot increase rent by more than the guideline of 2.1% per year, except in accordance with specific statutory exceptions. The proposed rent of $1021.00 (an increase from $1000.00) equals exactly 2.1% and complies with the guideline limit.","citation_ids":["39b1009bc8f5986ff6d23158c48054a70425f9a7e59c9afe73a0a9ae4599a723","f5c67f2bad6d3ffb8c81ecb8430050b1e1520e541055e34b8fea06961361f109"]},{"id":"s4","text":"Notice was served by hand on June 3, 2026, for an effective date of September 1, 2026, providing 90 days' notice as required. The 12-month spacing requirement is satisfied because the tenancy began September 1, 2025, and no previous rent increase occurred, so the increase on September 1, 2026, is at least 12 months from the tenancy start date.","citation_ids":["d08ffeb8ee9441f0852fa0b6039c56666890205c8a61f3aece4e90f8e8c23859","2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf"]},{"id":"s5","text":"Form N1 is the correct notice form for this rent increase because the rental unit is subject to the rent increase guideline under the Residential Tenancies Act and is not exempt or partially exempt.","citation_ids":["ef832f209b0ddce5e6d3d7d1172f5781b55cc9699aeb569c88ee258082bfa41e","416445ce84343a5b8e40226374c738b0b3a1e94663b5822e5b3b9b2cf08b88e5"]}],"tool_result":{"tool":"rent_increase_check","status":"passes_checked_rules","checks":[{"id":"scope","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["scope.ordinary"]},{"id":"supported_year","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":[]},{"id":"period_start","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["scope.ordinary"]},{"id":"notice","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["calendar.s89","notice.90_days"]},{"id":"spacing","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["calendar.s89","spacing.12_months"]},{"id":"guideline","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["guideline.2026"]},{"id":"form","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["form.N1"]}],"deemed_served_on":"2026-06-03","notice_day