{"run_id":"b0443699-0c37-4e10-a44f-712b80bdc4e7","case_id":"N01","arm":"production","repeat":0,"source_sha":"3d9299b01e973a843976ad13dcae9b544e217db4","config_hash":"45a78ce5322fc2605855a021e5b9ae0365f7e145712c4e1e6fc28151e70f071a","corpus_hash":"8be6cf66829e42c59cbec66896d1c762de130283989623a05edc30b427246720","gold_hash":"575cfd2b66465633f7cb04b44eb8455293c1a9c24a4911c0482879313d9b82cb","pricing_hash":"d1a472e5668c0455e1ff2e5a881403e9ccf01e148552153a757bd6c3aabd7f31","attempt_id":"79034ef7-5697-41ae-992a-10d10e9079c9","trace_ids":["80f4066e-cd0b-421c-9cc9-9dfc878fdcf1"],"retrieved_ids":["2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf","7621f3cdfae14d92b5777d44f620edbd19bd2126d494dddfc68261583181ea29","b0e650241164fd4a77ae93377f48b2b049494a5f55616d3b6f9ee8897fb1268b","78bdbb08ad03091d61e6fc6348085684e086003d7085608996508d9007fe5fe2","8c05df2155a417224adfe8d6373ad0fb66c46776574b5e633892ce52b5880502"],"foundation_evidence_ids":[],"initial_context_evidence_ids":["2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf","7621f3cdfae14d92b5777d44f620edbd19bd2126d494dddfc68261583181ea29","b0e650241164fd4a77ae93377f48b2b049494a5f55616d3b6f9ee8897fb1268b","78bdbb08ad03091d61e6fc6348085684e086003d7085608996508d9007fe5fe2","8c05df2155a417224adfe8d6373ad0fb66c46776574b5e633892ce52b5880502"],"response":{"attempt_id":"79034ef7-5697-41ae-992a-10d10e9079c9","trace_id":"80f4066e-cd0b-421c-9cc9-9dfc878fdcf1","status":"answered","answer":"A landlord must give a tenant at least 90 days' written notice before increasing rent. The notice must be in an approved form and set out the landlord's intention to increase the rent and the amount of the new rent.\nNotice can be given by handing it directly to the tenant or an adult in the rental unit, leaving it in the mailbox or where mail is ordinarily delivered, placing it under the door or through a mail slot, sending it by fax, courier, or mail. Notice cannot be given by posting it on the door.\nFor most rental units, a landlord cannot increase rent by more than the annual guideline set by the Ontario Government, which is based on the Consumer Price Index and capped at 2.5 percent. The guideline is published by August 31 for increases taking effect January 1 of the following year.\nRental units not occupied for residential purposes on or before November 15, 2018 may be exempt from the rent increase guideline. For such exempt units, a landlord must still give at least 90 days' notice but there is no limit on the size of the rent increase.\nThe notice served on June 3, 2026 for an effective date of September 1, 2026 satisfies the 90-day notice requirement. When counting days between two events, the day the first event happens is excluded and the day the second event happens is included.","statements":[{"id":"s1","text":"A landlord must give a tenant at least 90 days' written notice before increasing rent. The notice must be in an approved form and set out the landlord's intention to increase the rent and the amount of the new rent.","citation_ids":["9dc1e76e7aa94913d4d506df9ae748a0ae9b8de10a0a9253069dec8ea3bdc163"]},{"id":"s2","text":"Notice can be given by handing it directly to the tenant or an adult in the rental unit, leaving it in the mailbox or where mail is ordinarily delivered, placing it under the door or through a mail slot, sending it by fax, courier, or mail. Notice cannot be given by posting it on the door.","citation_ids":["2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf","7621f3cdfae14d92b5777d44f620edbd19bd2126d494dddfc68261583181ea29"]},{"id":"s3","text":"For most rental units, a landlord cannot increase rent by more than the annual guideline set by the Ontario Government, which is based on the Consumer Price Index and capped at 2.5 percent. The guideline is published by August 31 for increases taking effect January 1 of the following year.","citation_ids":["b0e650241164fd4a77ae93377f48b2b049494a5f55616d3b6f9ee8897fb1268b","39b1009bc8f5986ff6d23158c48054a70425f9a7e59c9afe73a0a9ae4599a723"]},{"id":"s4","text":"Rental units not occupied for residential purposes on or before November 15, 2018 may be exempt from the rent increase guideline. For such exempt units, a landlord must still give at least 90 days' notice but there is no limit on the size of the rent increase.","citation_ids":["b0e650241164fd4a77ae93377f48b2b049494a5f55616d3b6f9ee8897fb1268b"]},{"id":"s5","text":"The notice served on June 3, 2026 for an effective date of September 1, 2026 satisfies the 90-day notice requirement. When counting days between two events, the day the first event happens is excluded and the day the second event happens is included.","citation_ids":["d08ffeb8ee9441f0852fa0b6039c56666890205c8a61f3aece4e90f8e8c23859"]}],"tool_result":{"tool":"notice_deadline_check","status":"passes_checked_rules","checks":[{"id":"scope","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["scope.ordinary"]},{"id":"supported_year","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":[]},{"id":"period_start","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["scope.ordinary"]},{"id":"notice","status":"pass","reason":"satisfied","rule_ids":["calendar.s89","notice.90_days"]}],"deemed_served_on":"2026-06-03","notice_days":90,"earliest_notice_on":"2026-09-01","latest_deemed_service_on":"2026-06-03","latest_dispatch_on":"2026-06-03","earliest_spacing_on":null,"guideline_percent":null,"cap_cents_exact":null,"rule_ids":["calendar.s89","notice.90_days","scope.ordinary"]},"citations":[{"id":"2b1ee1673d0822c0043a1ecac510f46fa7825cd4b495c907a6adcceb577c01bf","url":"https://tribunalsontario.ca/documents/ltb/Notices%20of%20Rent%20Increase%20%26%20Instructions/N1%20instructions_final_Nov30_2015.pdf","heading":"N1 instructions — Section C: How to increase the rent and give this notice","snapshot_date":"2026-09-21"},{"id":"39b1009bc8f5986ff6d23158c48054a70425f9a7e59c9afe73a0a9ae4599a723","url":"https://www.ontario.ca/laws/statute/06r17","heading":"RTA s. 120 — Guideline